WEBVTT

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:02.980
.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.230
I'm Marty Stouper.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:10.250
We all think of ourselves as Americans,

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.370
but what does that really mean?

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:17.720
I'm part Norwegian, part Scotch-Irish, and part Greek.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.460
But I was born here,

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.340
and I would never want to live anywhere else.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.040
So I'm proud to call myself an American.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.310
This land first belonged to the Indians, though,

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.000
and to the world.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.350
wild creatures that roamed its forests, waters, and skies. The sight of a bald eagle gives

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:45.730
many of us a feeling of pride in the strength and freedom of our country. But what makes

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:54.780
a wild animal an American? Some have ancient claims to being original citizens. Others,

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.000
though we think of them as belonging here, are not natives. They're important.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.190
Just like most of us.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:09.920
I'd like to show you some of the fascinating creatures that I think of as all-American animals.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.550
Four continents as we know them had formed.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:30.000
Primitive life began in ancient seas.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:37.100
The first creatures that evolved were not very specialized but as climates

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:43.780
changed and land masses arose slowly each continent developed its own unique

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:51.040
plants and animals. First reptiles then finally mammals ruled our North American

00:02:51.040 --> 00:03:00.000
continent. Most early species have long since disappeared but the pronghorn has been fully evolved

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:08.520
for almost 2 million years.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.920
And they are still with us today, America's oldest natives,

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:20.550
unrelated to any other animal.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.410
The pronghorn's main secret of survival

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.900
is its phenomenal speed, up to 70 miles an hour.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.960
No animal in North America

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:49.650
and few anywhere can match it.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.180
Males and females have horns, which they shed each year.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.680
They're the only horned animals to do so.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.000
The males sometimes use theirs in play

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.900
sparring contests. If the bald eagle and the pronghorn both seem like natural

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:14.710
all-American contenders, one of the more unlikely candidates is the opossum,

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:22.040
sometimes called the Virginia opossum or usually just possum. It's found from

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.770
Florida northward through all the eastern states into New Hampshire and

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:30.000
Maine. It's been introduced as far away

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:35.040
as Washington and Oregon, and its range is still expanding.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.680
Though its food habits bring it close to man,

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.680
it goes unnoticed because it is active mainly at night.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:53.720
During the day, its den is often in a hollow tree,

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.360
such as this one in Arkansas.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.000
Although not a high-flyer or a swift-runner,

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.530
the opossum has many characteristics

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.440
that make its life story as fascinating

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.580
as that of any other American animal.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.000
For one thing,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
the list of North American mammals

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.000
as our only marsupial.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.490
Like Australia's kangaroo

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.420
and koala,

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.470
the female possum has a pouch

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.450
in which her young finish

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:46.480
their early development.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.890
Aww.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:53.640
For another, the hind feet

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.640
have a clawless big toe

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:57.540
which is opposable,

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.000
just like our thumb.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.060
are the only non-primate which have this feature.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:09.000
They also have 50 teeth, more than any other mammal.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:45.200
With all these teeth showing, it's hard to tell if this behavior is playing or fighting,

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.480
but during the mating season, as with most animals, there's usually a little of both.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:13.100
The scientific name of the opossum family is didelphus, which means double wound. In marsupials,

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:21.610
the rectum and vagina are combined into a single opening. The female's two oviducts branch out to

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:30.000
the right and left sides of this cloaca. This accounts for the unusual forked shape nature has

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.760
given to the male's penis.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:16.260
Stories from old folklore tell us that mating took place in the female's nostrils and that

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:20.320
she later breathed her babies into her pouch.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:25.420
Like most old wives tales, this one is obviously not true.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:30.000
Possums breed the same way that most other mammals do, and the male's penis is the same

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:33.260
is shaped to serve the females double uterus.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:41.660
Over the southern part of its range opossums produce two litters each year.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:47.700
The breeding season begins in January and may last into late September.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.540
They are polygamous and the male will breed with any female

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:58.040
that he finds receptive. Opossums are normally very cautious

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.700
and slow-moving animals.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:05.370
This is about as active as I've ever seen them.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:18.970
With their opposable toes and prehensile tails,

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:23.510
they're as much at home in the treetops as they are on the ground.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:25.640
That is, most of the time.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:09.960
With an extremely short gestation period of 13 days,

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.160
it's time to find a safe den.

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:52.880
The pouch is cleaned as the female senses the onset of birth.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:10.920
For a mother opossum, labor is not long or difficult.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:15.970
Marsupial young are born while still in an embryonic stage of development.

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:46.250
These tiny babies are smaller than honey bees.

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.910
As many as 20 of them may be born in a few minutes.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:19.720
They look so helpless.

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:24.630
How will they ever reach the safe destination of their mother's pouch?

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:30.000
Unlike other mammals, which are born attached to an umbilical cord, the young possum

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.090
are floating free inside the uterus the mucus layer could suffocate them now

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:48.190
instinctively the mother licks it away at this stage the hind legs are hardly

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:53.610
present but the tiny forelegs are already well developed complete with

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:59.250
claws that may aid in reaching the pouch some won't make it

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:30.000
until quite recently it was thought that

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.510
the young were born directly into the pouch, or that the mother used her mouth to put them

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:42.470
there. But now we know that the young emerge with the unique instinct to migrate, hand

00:13:42.470 --> 00:14:00.000
over hand, through the mother's fur, their longest journey. Here it's

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:07.860
first come first served. There are only 13 nipples in the pouch and only 13 babies will

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:19.140
find a place in this natural incubator to feed and grow. There is no taking turns. Each

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:27.200
nipple swells to fill the mouth of the baby lucky enough to find it. The young become firmly

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:42.550
here they stay fastened for about four or five weeks. Special muscles allow the

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:56.280
mother to close up her pouch like a purse. By the end of two months the young

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:00.000
have opened their eyes, grown fur, and are about the same

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.170
size of mice. Though still dependent on the mother, they now venture outside the

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:16.890
pouch, all of them trying to crowd back in at once, if frightened. As they grow

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:22.020
older and braver, they begin to ride around on the outside, clinging to their

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:22.660
mother's fur.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:48.910
Gradually, some of them get left behind.

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:53.460
At three months of age, ready or not, they're on their own.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:24.820
Among my favorite all-American birds is the scissor-tailed flycatcher.

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:30.000
Its breeding grounds are in our central and southwestern states, but it has been known

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:45.440
wander as far east as Massachusetts whether courting catching insects on the

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.720
wing or just soaring for the playful pleasure of it these birds are true

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:54.520
artists of the air and true Americans

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:52.890
Many of the living things that we think of as native

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:55.170
are actually imports.

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:56.240
Tumbleweed,

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:57.850
or Russian thistle,

00:17:57.850 --> 00:17:59.670
has rolled over our plains

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:08.260
since it was introduced by settlers traveling west in covered wagons some

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:12.870
animal immigrants like the ring-neck pheasant have become almost as much

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:18.500
natives as their predators these coyotes have caught the scent of something

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:21.920
appetizing and they're not about to give up easily

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:36.810
Many species have been turned loose in this country to provide sport for hunters.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:45.240
Sometimes they quickly die out, but sometimes they threaten or even displace native populations.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:52.370
The ring-necked pheasant has thrived to fill ecological niches left vacant by native game birds

00:19:52.370 --> 00:20:00.000
that had already been decimated by man, and it provides good eating for the coyote, prairie falcon,

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:22.890
and badger. Toughest all-American animal, my vote would have to go to the peccary of our

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:30.000
southwestern states. Any creature that can thrive on this kind of diet shows a lot of native

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:53.720
These wild hogs, on the other hand, are not natives at all. Descendants of

00:20:53.720 --> 00:21:00.000
animals that escaped from farmers or were released. They infest the woodlands of Kentucky

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.960
Tennessee, the Carolinas, and other southeastern states. Their constant

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:12.910
rooting can do considerable damage to forests and fields. Because we have

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:18.730
killed off all our large predators like cougars and wolves in the east, they have

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:30.000
almost no natural enemies. Even the male bobcat is too small

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:49.140
be a serious threat to these half-grown piglets. It seems cautious as if it knows

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:53.970
all about their thick hides and about the mother's sharp teeth.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:28.360
meanwhile the female bobcat approaches the problem from a different angle

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:30.000
but not even these normally

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.480
fierce cats are willing to test the mother hog's protective instincts.

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:46.640
there are other conflicts between natives and newcomers

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:48.930
beautiful as swans are

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:51.150
some of them are creating problems

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:55.240
for original american species

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:57.110
these are mute swans

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:58.850
natives of europe

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:00.000
introduced to america

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.800
during colonial days they have established themselves as lords of the

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:10.950
lake in New England and New Jersey in Michigan and here on the Chesapeake Bay

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:12.200
in Maryland

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:39.530
more aggressive than other swans they drive out the native whistling or tundra swans that preceded

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:46.880
them here by many generations their long necks allow them to feed underwater to a depth that

00:24:46.880 --> 00:25:00.000
leaves other waterfowl with little to eat they are also more prolific than the other swans and where

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.370
successful populations, they usually have done so at the expense of other

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:15.510
wildlife, ducks and geese especially, and even turtles, fish and frogs. Further west,

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:21.920
where mute swans have not intruded, the call of our native swans still echoes

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:23.880
over cold water lakes.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:35.990
America is composed of many nationalities, which we welcome with open arms.

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:44.010
But in the case of wildlife, we should remember that it has taken many, many centuries for our native species

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:51.960
to achieve the balance that gives America such a splendid and fragile wild heritage.

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:51.490
I've lived with wildlife in many countries, in Africa, Europe, even in the South Pacific.

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:58.150
From the most rugged, dirty bush camps to the fanciest hotels, I've seen a lot of places.

00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:00.000
But for me, there really is.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:04.510
no place like home from sea to sea

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:08.310
America's mountains deserts forests

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:11.460
and planes are as magnificent as

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:17.410
any on earth there are many reasons to appreciate our country

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:22.720
one of the things I love most about it is the special beauty

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:27.700
and rich variety of our all-american animals

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:30.000
I'm Marty Stauffer

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:34.440
Until next time, enjoy our wild America.
